#!/usr/bin/env etcs

# A window, its input edge, a scene, and a rendering edge -- then the script
# ENDS, on purpose, and you land in the ETCS shell with all of it still live.
#
# Run it with the interactive build (`ace make loader`, which is the one that
# gets -DETCS_REPL_SHELL) from the ETCS root:
#
#     ./bin/etcs scripts/scene_demo.etcs
#
# and you get a `Root>` prompt with a window still on screen and still
# presenting -- measured at 864 frames in 14.6s, 59.3 fps, while sitting idle
# at the prompt. From there:
#
#     Root> RenderProvider                # navigate: tags, live counts, and
#                                         #   this script's names with RIDs
#     Root> jobs                          # the two detached edges, by id
#     Root> scripts/scene_layer.etcs view=<RID> backdrop=<RID>
#     Root> scripts/scene_reset.etcs view=<RID> backdrop=<RID>
#     Root> scripts/scene_pump.etcs  main=<RID>
#     Root> signal 2 interrupt            # stop the frame edge, keep the shell
#
# The RIDs come from the navigator, which prints them under "Root script names
# (reachable by attach/ensure)". They are handed to the follow-up scripts as
# INJECTED names rather than attached ones -- see scene_layer.etcs for why a
# script started from the prompt cannot simply attach a previous root's
# globals, and why that refusal is correct.
#
# WHY THE LAST EDGE IS DETACHED. A script that ends on a blocking call owns
# the process for as long as it runs -- main.Run() is that, and it is the
# right shape for an application. This is the other shape: every long-lived
# thing is handed to a detached member, the script's own lines run out, and
# the SHELL becomes the outermost scope. That is not the script leaking, it
# is the closure changing hands: the shell's SignalContext is the global-most
# authority now, and Ctrl+C at the prompt is what ends the closure the script
# opened. `jobs` lists its members; `signal <id> interrupt` stops one.
#
# THE THREAD LEDGER, and the one caveat worth knowing before you start:
#
#   1. this script's thread    -- runs the lines below, then becomes the
#                                 shell's prompt loop
#   2. a ThreadPool worker     -- Window.ProduceEvents, held for the window's
#                                 lifetime
#   3. a detached thread       -- Window.ConsumeEvents, blocked on the input
#                                 stream
#   4. a ThreadPool worker     -- Surface.ProduceFrames, the frame clock
#   5. a detached thread       -- Surface.ConsumeFrames, every Vulkan call
#
# THE CAVEAT: GLFW binds event polling to the thread that created the window,
# which is thread 1 -- and thread 1 is at the prompt. So nothing is calling
# glfwPollEvents while you are typing: FRAMES KEEP PRESENTING (that is thread
# 5, and Vulkan is happy to present from a thread that did not create the
# window), but keystrokes into the window and the close button do not arrive
# until something polls. Run
#
#     Root> scripts/scene_pump.etcs main=<RID>
#
# to service the queue once, from the correct thread. Living with that is the
# price of having a prompt; main.Run() is the trade in the other direction.

spawn RenderProvider::Instance gpu
gpu.Create()

# Create, not Run: Run would enter the poll loop and never come back, and the
# Surface cannot be built until the window exists -- it takes its VkSurfaceKHR
# from the window's native handle and its extent from the window's size.
spawn WindowProvider::Window main

main.Run(1024, 768, 'ETCS scene')

# Input first: ProduceEvents spins on IsActive() before registering an
# observer, so detaching it before the window is live just burns thread 2
# waiting.
detach window_events.etcs window=main

# Child, not a root spawn: the parent link IS the binding between a surface
# and the window it draws into.
main.spawn(RenderProvider::Surface view)
view.Create(@gpu, modules/RenderProvider/shaders/)

# ── the scene ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# CPU layers, composited on the CPU, then blitted to the screen as one image.
# Root spawns: a layer has no owner in the scene-graph sense yet -- it is an
# addressable region of pixels whose RID the thing compositing knows. That
# changes when the Drawable families get a concrete leaf; until then the
# scene's structure lives in this file rather than in the entity tree, which
# is exactly what scene_layer.etcs has to work around.
spawn RenderProvider::ImageSurface backdrop
backdrop.Create(640, 480)
backdrop.Clear(0.10, 0.12, 0.20, 1.0)
backdrop.DrawRect(40, 40, 240, 140, 0.85, 0.72, 0.20, 1.0)
backdrop.DrawRect(320, 260, 260, 180, 0.20, 0.65, 0.85, 1.0)

spawn RenderProvider::ImageSurface badge
badge.Create(160, 160)
badge.Clear(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)
badge.DrawRect(0, 0, 160, 60, 0.90, 0.25, 0.35, 0.75)

# Layer onto layer, on the CPU.
backdrop.Blit(@badge, 380, 60, 0, 0, 0.85)

# Layer onto the screen, on the GPU: same verb, different surface.
view.Clear(0.03, 0.03, 0.05, 1.0)
view.Blit(@backdrop, 80, 60, 864, 648, 1.0)

# And the frame pump. This is the last line that starts anything, and after
# it the script has nothing left to do -- which is the whole point.
detach render_frames.etcs view=view
